 texture and anisotropy drive separation\",\"explanation\":\"CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC TEXTURE develops in controlled-rolled steel, and its magnitude depends on deformation in the non-recrystallization or two-phase region and on the transformed microstructure. It causes planar anisotropies in mechanical properties, and texture development causes anisotropy; this anisotropy is considered the most serious disadvantage of controlled-rolled steel. {100} TEXTURE, described as 〈100〉ND, mainly causes through-thickness embrittlement. Separation occurs only when fracture strength is anisotropic, and anisotropy in fracture strength controls separation. [Data: Entities (34, 35, 626, 627, 706, 670, 754); Relationships (31, 1202, 1288, 1377, 1320, 1478, +more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Band density and {100} intensity jointly determine separation occurrence\",\"explanation\":\"Occurrence of separation is determined by the combined effect of band density and {100} intensity. Development of {100} texture in C-Mn steel produces a large effect of separation and a decrease in transition temperature; the extent of separation increases with increasing {100} intensity. Banded structure is a microstructure along which initiated separation tends to propagate, and Cr disperses or suppresses banded structure, thereby preventing separations. [Data: Entities (801, 802, 816, 914, 746, 803, 998); Relationships (1579, 1580, 1885, 1581, 2076, +more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"MnS inclusions initiate cracks and define propagation paths\",\"explanation\":\"Flattened MnS inclusions trigger crack initiation, and low-temperature finish rolling flattens MnS inclusions. Cracks initiate at MnS inclusions or weak locations and propagate along grains with {100} orientation and/or banded structures; pearlite bands are also paths. Clusters of crystal grains with {100} orientation are main sources of separation cracks. When MnS is only occasional, as with extremely low S content, separation can initiate at the second weakest location. [Data: Entities (785, 743, 819, 779, 773, 748, 750, 774, 784, 820); Relationships (1551, 1450, 1449, 1520, 1522, 1454, 1455, 1550, +more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Processing and transformed microstructure produce susceptible structures\",\"explanation\":\"Conventional rolling of coarse γ produces a transformed structure containing coarse α and bainite, a mixture that deteriorates notch toughness. Range 1 is defined by deformation above 1000°C and involves coarse recrystallized γ-grains; Type 1A is equivalent to range 1. Coarse recrystallized γ-grains transform to relatively coarse α and upper bainitic structure, and γ-grain size ASTM 5 transforms to relatively coarse α. Bainite, alone or with banded structure, can cause through-thickness embrittlement. [Data: Entities (101, 128, 129, 131, 135, 182, 184, 213, 242, 246); Relationships (118, 133, 134, 200, 241, 244, 307, 350, 357, 379, +more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Mitigation targets banding, MnS shape, and sulphur content\",\"explanation\":\"Cr in controlled-rolled steel disperses banded structure and suppresses separation, while having little effect on controlled-rolled properties; addition of Cr can disperse or suppress banded structure, preventing separations. Inclusion-shape control is employed to eliminate flattened MnS by converting elongated sulphide to globular inclusion using rare earth metals and/or Ca. A new desulphurization method developed by advancing sulphide-shape control can reduce S content to extremely low levels. These measures target elimination and prevention of separations. [Data: Entities (803, 791, 793, 794, 795, 797, 814, 815); Relationships (1581, 1564, 1566, 1567, 1568, 1570, 1569, 1608, 1609, 1610, +more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Charpy testing reveals stress-state and fracture-temperature effects\",\"explanation\":\"Separations occur in a brittle manner in a fractured Charpy specimen, parallel to the plate surface, and are greatest near the temperature where the ductile-fracture area becomes just 100%